      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/trichopsis-schalleri-2/",
      "html": "<p>Schaller's Croaking Gourami attains a length of about 4 cm. It becomes larger than the Croaking Dwarf Gourami (Trichopsis pumila, 2.5-3 cm), but stays smaller than the common Croaking Gourami (T. vittata, up to 6 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/trichopsis-schalleri-2/",
      "html": "<p>Schaller's Croaking Gourami is sadly only very occasionally available in the trade.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/trichopsis-schalleri-2/",
      "html": "<p>The fish originates from central Thailand.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/trichopsis-schalleri-2/",
      "html": "<p>These are very undemanding fish that are ideal inhabitants for community tanks with small, peaceful fish from Southeastern Asia, like Rasboras.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/trichopsis-schalleri-2/",
      "html": "<p>In contrast to many other labyrinth fishes, the males prefer to build the bubble nest under broad leaves, in caves or similar places instead of on the water surface.</p>"
    }
